## 🛡️ Polling Best Practices for Autonomous Agents

   1. Enforce Intent-Driven Backoff: When designing your polling scripts, use exponential backoff logic (e.g., doubling the wait time up to a maximum limit if an endpoint returns a 429 Too Many Requests status code). This prevents your infrastructure from getting blacklisted.
   2. Abstract Identity with Clean HTTP Headers: Always declare a distinct, non-generic User-Agent within your request configurations. Many web3 and data routing networks block naked connections to prevent malicious botting.
   3. Utilize ETag and Conditional Headers: When tracking high-frequency feeds, store the ETag or Last-Modified string returned by the server. In subsequent requests, include an If-None-Match or If-Modified-Since header. If no new data has been added, the server returns a lightweight 304 Not Modified payload, bypassing rate-limit meters and saving heavy computing cycles.

